course: everyday
lesson: 428
title: The broken window and other hidden accidents
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Owen's football smashed Mr Patel's greenhouse in Leeds. He and his mate legged it (ran off) and hid behind the shed. When Mr Patel came round, Owen said «Weren't us, we were inside.» His football was lying among the broken glass — with his name written on it in marker. Mum paid for the glass from his birthday money, and Owen had to say sorry to Mr Patel face to face, which felt worse than the glass.
explain: An accident is not a lie. Hiding it is. Ben's stone bounced off a wall and broke a school window; he walked away and told the dinner lady «No, miss». The camera showed it all. The head said: «A bounced stone is an accident. Walking away and lying is a choice.» He got a detention — for the lie, not the window.
explain: Hidden accidents send people chasing the wrong problem. Dylan sat on the TV remote, taped it up and said «the batteries must be dead»; Dad bought batteries for nothing. «The remote was a fiver. The lie was a whole trip to the shop.» Finn dropped Dad's phone in the bath, dried it with a hair dryer and blamed «the update»; the repair shop said «water damage», and saying «it got wet» at once might have saved it.
explain: Some hide-and-blame stories hurt others. Aroha in Wellington cracked a lampshade trampolining in the lounge and blamed the cat; Mum said «The cat was at the vet all day. Try again.» Aroha lost trampoline time for a week. Chloe in Pittsburgh hid a nail-polish stain under a rug and told her brother «yinz didn't see anything» («yinz» is Pittsburgh for «you all») — so he got in trouble too. Mia hid Gran's broken china cat; Gran wasn't sad about the cat, only that Mia hadn't told her, and gave her a cwtch, the Welsh word for a cuddle.
explain: Isla in Edinburgh broke her friend's pen and slipped it back into the pencil case; the next day she said «Aye, it was me, sorry, I'll get you a new one», and her friend was mad for a day, then fine. Kai in Sydney showed the other way too. A ball broke the Wilsons' window and three boys ran. Kai went back alone and knocked: «G'day, that was me, sorry.» Mr Wilson, about to call the police, shook his hand, and Kai washed his car three times to help pay. The other two were found from a neighbour's camera and were in far more trouble.
fact: An accident is not a lie; hiding it or blaming someone else is.
fact: The lie about an accident is often punished more than the accident itself.
fact: Many places, like schools and shops, have cameras that record what happens.
fact: Evidence like a named ball, a taped remote or a stain usually stays behind after an accident.
fact: Hiding a broken thing makes people chase the wrong problem, like buying new batteries for a broken remote.
fact: Electronics that get wet can sometimes be saved if a grown-up knows straight away.
fact: Blaming a pet or a baby who cannot answer back is a cowardly lie and often fails.
fact: Pulling someone else into your secret makes them carry your lie and share your trouble.
fact: Grown-ups often forgive accidents quickly but remember being lied to.
fact: Coming back to say «it was me» is hard and brave, and people treat the one who owns up very differently.
fact: Helping to pay for or fix damage, from pocket money or jobs, is a fair way to put an accident right.
fact: «Legged it» (UK) means ran away; «cwtch» (Wales) means a cuddle; «yinz» (Pittsburgh) means «you all».
exercise: What did Owen say when Mr Patel came round? | «Weren't us, we were inside» | «Sorry, Mr Patel, it was my ball» | «My mate kicked it, not me» | «A bird must have broken it»
exercise: What clue lay among the broken glass? | Owen's football with his name on it | Owen's left shoe and a muddy sock | A note from Owen's mate saying sorry | Owen's school bag
exercise: What did Owen find hardest in the end? | Saying sorry to Mr Patel face to face | Paying for the glass from his birthday money | Sweeping up the glass | Finding a new football
exercise: Why did Gran feel sad about her china cat? | Mia hadn't told her it broke | The cat was very expensive to replace | Mia glued it back the wrong way round | Gran wanted a new cat to replace it
exercise: What did Dad buy because of Dylan's lie? | New batteries that didn't help | A new television for the front room | A new sofa cushion | Nothing at all
exercise: Why did Chloe's brother get in trouble too? | She made him keep her secret | He spilled the polish on the rug | He moved the rug over the stain | He laughed at the stain and told Mum
exercise: Ben's stone broke a window by accident. What was his detention for? | Walking away and lying about it | The broken window itself, and the cost | Throwing stones near a wall | Talking to the dinner lady
exercise: How did Aroha's mum know the cat didn't do it? | The cat was at the vet all day | The cat can't jump that high up | Aroha had paw prints on her | The neighbour saw the cat
exercise: What did Aroha lose for the trampolining and the lie? | Trampoline time for a week | Her cat sleeping on her bed for a week | Her bedroom lamp | Nothing, Mum let it go
exercise: What did the repair shop find in Dad's phone? | Water damage | A software update | A broken screen | A flat battery
exercise: Why might telling Dad at once have helped the phone? | Wet electronics can sometimes be saved quickly | Phones dry out better in the dark, left in a drawer | Dad could blame the update | Hair dryers fix all phones
exercise: How did Isla's friend react when Isla owned up about the pen? | Mad for a day, then fine | Never spoke to her again after that | Laughed and said it was nothing | Told the whole school about it
exercise: What did Kai do after the ball broke the Wilsons' window? | Went back alone and said it was him | Ran off down the road with the others | Blamed the other two boys | Left a note with no name
exercise: How were the two boys who ran found out? | A neighbour's camera | Kai told on them | They left their ball | Mr Wilson knew their mums
exercise: How did Kai help put it right? | Washed Mr Wilson's car three times | Bought him a brand new window with his savings | Gave him his football | Did nothing, saying sorry was enough
exercise: Why do grown-ups forgive accidents more easily than lies? | Accidents happen to everyone, but lies are a choice | Lies cost more money than accidents | Accidents are always cheap | Grown-ups never forgive anything
test: Mate: «leg it, nobody saw!!» What does «leg it» mean? | Run away | Kick the ball | Stay still | Hide the leg
test: Chloe: «Yinz didn't see anything, right?» Who is she talking to? | Her brother — «yinz» means «you all» | Her mum, who is on the phone in the kitchen | Her cat | A teacher
test: «it's the update, phone's been weird all day». What will a repair shop find? | Water damage from the bath | A bad update that broke the phone | Nothing wrong at all | A broken hair dryer
test: Ben, scared: «I'll get expelled for a window!» What did the head actually say? | The accident is small; walking away and lying was the choice | Windows mean instant expulsion | Bounced stones are always on purpose | He'd never find out who did it
test: Gran gives Mia a cwtch and says «never hide things from me, my lover». What is a cwtch? | A cuddle | A telling-off | A glue pot | A biscuit
test: What would happen if Kai had run off with the others? | He'd likely be caught by the camera and in more trouble | Nobody would ever know | Mr Wilson would thank him anyway | The window would fix itself
test: Kid: «just blame the cat, it can't argue lol». What's the truth? | It's a coward's lie, and it often fails anyway | Cats always get the blame fairly | Parents can never check where the cat was all day | It's fine if it's a small thing
test: Isla broke her friend's pen and slipped it back. Next day the friend looks straight at her. Best move? | «Aye, it was me, sorry, I'll get you a new one» | «Dunno, maybe it was already broken when I had it» | Look away and say nothing | Say someone else had it first
test: Dylan: «If I say nothing, the remote just died, right?» Why is this a problem? | Dad chases the wrong fix and wastes money | Remotes never break | Dad will never notice the tape on the back | It's always the batteries
test: In a cartoon, a kid hides a broken vase and gets away with it forever. Which is closer to real life? | Broken things are usually found, and the hiding hurts more | Broken things are never found | Hiding is always the smart choice | Grans never dust
test: Owen's mum paid for the glass from his birthday money. Why is that fair? | He caused the damage, so he helps pay | Mums always want birthday money for themselves | Mr Patel asked for a present | It wasn't fair, it was an accident
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil who, having broken a window accidentally, departed and denied knowledge thereof. | The denial, not the accident, constituted the principal offence | The accident constituted the principal offence | Neither act warranted any consequence | The denial was justified by fear
exam: Identify the principal reason for which concealment of damage to an electronic device is inadvisable. | Prompt disclosure of water damage may permit the device to be saved | Electronic devices repair themselves if left alone | Concealment reduces the cost of repair | Repair shops cannot detect water damage
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of damage to an animal which was absent at the material time? | It is a false accusation readily disproved by the facts | It is an acceptable explanation | It is honest provided the animal is frequently mischievous | It removes all responsibility from the child
exam: Determine the conduct most likely to be treated leniently by a neighbour whose window has been broken by a ball. | The prompt return of the responsible child to admit the damage | The departure of all children concerned | The attribution of the damage to another child | The leaving of an anonymous note
